sections.body:
Before Java 7, closing a resource safely was an error-prone routine: open it, wrap the usage in try/finally, and null-check it inside finally before calling close(), because the open itself might have failed.

```java
BufferedReader reader = null;
try {
    reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path));
    return reader.readLine();
} finally {
    if (reader != null) {
        reader.close();   // close() can ALSO throw IOException
    }
}
```

This has two problems beyond extra typing. First, the null check exists because the constructor might have thrown before `reader` was assigned, and forgetting it risks a `NullPointerException` inside `finally` on top of the original failure. Second, and more damaging: if the `try` block throws (say, an I/O failure while reading) and `close()` inside `finally` *also* throws, the exception from `close()` replaces the original one. The root cause is lost, and whoever catches the exception sees only the usually less informative `close()` failure.

Java 7 added a form of `try` that declares one or more resources in parentheses after the keyword. Each resource is closed automatically at the end of the block, whether it completes normally or exits with an exception, and no `finally` is needed for that.

```java
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) {
    return reader.readLine();
}   // reader.close() is called automatically here, no matter how the block exits
```

Any type used this way must implement `java.lang.AutoCloseable` (or its subinterface `Closeable`, used throughout the I/O classes, which narrows `close()`'s declared exception to `IOException`). The compiler refuses a try-with-resources over a type that doesn't.

Multiple resources can be declared in the same statement, separated by semicolons, and they're closed **in reverse order of declaration**:

```java
try (var connection = openConnection();
     var statement = connection.createStatement()) {
    // use statement, which depends on connection being open
}   // statement is closed FIRST, then connection -- reverse of declaration order
```

The order is not arbitrary. A resource declared later often depends on an earlier one still being open while it is torn down (closing a `Statement` after its `Connection` is already closed could misbehave), so closing in reverse tears down the most recently acquired, most dependent resource first.

try-with-resources also fixes the lost-exception problem. If the try body throws *and* the automatic close() also throws, the body's exception is the **primary** exception and is the one propagated to the caller. The exception from close() is not discarded: it is attached to the primary one as a **suppressed exception**, and `getSuppressed()` on the primary exception returns an array of those attached exceptions.

```java
class FlakyResource implements AutoCloseable {
    void doWork() { throw new RuntimeException("work failed"); }
    public void close() { throw new IllegalStateException("close failed too"); }
}

try (FlakyResource r = new FlakyResource()) {
    r.doWork();
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    System.out.println("primary: " + e.getMessage());              // "work failed"
    System.out.println("suppressed: " + e.getSuppressed()[0].getMessage()); // "close failed too"
}
```

If the try body completes *without* throwing but close() throws, that close() exception propagates. No suppression is involved, since there is no earlier exception for it to defer to.

Implementing `AutoCloseable` requires one method, `void close() throws Exception`. A class may narrow the declared exception, including declaring none, because an overriding method may narrow but never widen its declared checked exceptions.

```java
class SimpleConnection implements AutoCloseable {
    private final String name;

    SimpleConnection(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("opening " + name);
    }

    void query(String sql) {
        System.out.println(name + " running: " + sql);
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("closing " + name);
    }
}

try (SimpleConnection conn = new SimpleConnection("db1")) {
    conn.query("SELECT 1");
}
// prints: opening db1 / db1 running: SELECT 1 / closing db1
```

Since Java 9, the resource no longer has to be a *new* declaration inside the parentheses. It can be an existing effectively final variable already in scope: `try (conn) { ... }`. It must still be effectively final (never reassigned after its first assignment), because Java needs one value to close, fixed once, not a variable that might point somewhere else by the time the block ends. When you write your own `close()`, make it safe to call twice if you can: `Closeable` requires that, and the `AutoCloseable` documentation strongly advises it, because a resource can be closed explicitly inside the block and then closed again automatically at the end.

examples.code:
// BEFORE: manual cleanup, with a null check needed and a lost-exception risk
BufferedReader reader = null;
try {
    reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"));
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
} finally {
    if (reader != null) {
        reader.close();   // if this throws, it can silently replace an exception from readLine()
    }
}

// AFTER: try-with-resources handles both problems automatically
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
}

class Resource implements AutoCloseable {
    private final String name;
    Resource(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("open " + name);
    }
    @Override
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("close " + name);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Resource a = new Resource("A");
             Resource b = new Resource("B");
             Resource c = new Resource("C")) {
            System.out.println("using A, B, C");
        }
    }
}

class FlakyResource implements AutoCloseable {
    void doWork() {
        throw new RuntimeException("work failed");
    }
    @Override
    public void close() {
        throw new IllegalStateException("close failed too");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (FlakyResource r = new FlakyResource()) {
            r.doWork();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("caught: " + e.getMessage());
            for (Throwable suppressed : e.getSuppressed()) {
                System.out.println("  suppressed: " + suppressed.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}

